WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.743
RNA-protein interactions drive gene regulation,

00:00:03.823 --> 00:00:07.208
yet measuring them in the lab is slow and costly,

00:00:07.288 --> 00:00:11.803
and existing predictors lean on small,
protein-specific datasets.

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:14.137
This work introduces RNAInterAct,

00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:19.793
a large curated dataset of non-coding RNA-protein
interactions, and RPIembeddor,

00:00:19.873 --> 00:00:26.863
a transformer model that classifies whether any RNA
and protein interact using only their sequences.

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:30.328
By feeding embeddings from two foundation models,

00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:35.348
RNA-FM for RNA and ESM-2 for proteins, into an
attention-based network,

00:00:35.428 --> 00:00:43.620
RPIembeddor outperforms prior state-of-the-art
methods and generalizes to unseen RNA families and data distributions.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.322
Non-coding RNAs regulate the cell largely through
their interactions with proteins,

00:00:49.402 --> 00:00:53.552
but mapping these interactions experimentally is
slow and costly.

00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:59.605
Most computational predictors sidestep the general
problem by training one model per protein,

00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:04.096
which requires a large interaction dataset for that
specific protein.

00:01:04.176 --> 00:01:10.474
Such datasets exist for only a few hundred of the
roughly two thousand human RNA-binding proteins.

00:01:10.554 --> 00:01:15.485
What is missing is a method that decides, for any
given RNA and protein pair,

00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:19.130
whether they interact using nothing but their sequences.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.158
Recent progress shows two useful ideas.

00:01:23.238 --> 00:01:26.886
First, learning across many tasks, rather than one
protein at a time,

00:01:26.966 --> 00:01:30.613
can help when labeled data is scarce.

00:01:30.693 --> 00:01:34.341
Second,

00:01:34.421 --> 00:01:38.069
foundation models trained on huge unlabeled
biological corpora capture structural and

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:41.797
functional signal that raw sequences do not expose
directly.

00:01:41.877 --> 00:01:45.524
Combining these ideas,

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:49.252
a single model could learn general rules of
RNA-protein binding and apply them to interaction types it has never seen,

00:01:49.332 --> 00:01:52.980
which is exactly what a broad, sequence-only
predictor needs.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.569
The work makes three contributions.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:57.008
It builds RNAInterAct,

00:01:57.088 --> 00:02:02.568
an extensive dataset of non-coding RNA-protein
interactions derived from the RNAInter

00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:06.297
database and enriched with carefully generated negatives.

00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:07.998
It introduces RPIembeddor,

00:02:08.078 --> 00:02:13.623
a transformer that classifies interactions from
sequence embeddings and beats existing

00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:16.043
tools while generalizing to new data.

00:02:16.123 --> 00:02:21.734
And through an ablation study it shows that the two
foundation-model embeddings are not

00:02:21.814 --> 00:02:26.640
optional add-ons but the core of the model's
ability to classify correctly.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:32.910
RPIembeddor turns sequences into knowledge by
leaning on two pre-trained foundation models.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.469
RNA sequences are embedded with RNA-FM,

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:40.848
trained on twenty-three million non-coding RNAs,
and protein sequences with ESM-2,

00:02:40.928 --> 00:02:44.784
which predicts folding without multiple sequence
alignments.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:48.393
Both produce embeddings of size N by six hundred forty.

00:02:48.473 --> 00:02:52.001
Two parallel feed-forward layers normalize their sizes,

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:57.774
and encoder layers process the RNA and protein
embeddings symmetrically so attention can

00:02:57.854 --> 00:03:02.039
focus on the parts of each sequence most relevant
to interaction.

00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:08.009
The latent representations are concatenated and
passed through further feed-forward layers,

00:03:08.089 --> 00:03:13.519
ending in a linear layer with a sigmoid that
outputs the probability of interaction.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:17.390
The whole model has just one-point-four million
parameters.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.625
The dataset is the backbone of the study.

00:03:20.705 --> 00:03:25.706
Starting from RNAInter, with over forty-seven
million RNA interactions,

00:03:25.786 --> 00:03:31.218
the authors recover sequences by cross-referencing
NCBI, UniProt and Ensembl,

00:03:31.298 --> 00:03:35.655
and assign RNA families from Rfam and protein clans
from Pfam.

00:03:35.735 --> 00:03:42.669
Those annotations let them generate negatives that
are biologically meaningful rather than random.

00:03:42.749 --> 00:03:48.753
The final RNAInterAct set holds about one hundred
twenty-two thousand interactions at

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:51.616
a one-to-two positive-to-negative ratio.

00:03:51.696 --> 00:03:54.264
Crucially, it is split by RNA family,

00:03:54.344 --> 00:03:57.843
so no family appears in both training and testing.

00:03:57.923 --> 00:04:03.211
This homology-aware split, plus evaluation on the
external RPI2825 dataset,

00:04:03.291 --> 00:04:06.790
tests true generalization instead of memorization.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.998
On the hardest test, TSfam, where no RNA family
overlaps with training,

00:04:12.078 --> 00:04:19.462
RPIembeddor reaches an F1 score of about
zero-point-five-nine and an accuracy of about zero-point-six-seven.

00:04:19.542 --> 00:04:23.195
Its ROC area under the curve is zero-point-seven-zero,

00:04:23.275 --> 00:04:29.900
while the competing tools XRPI and IPMiner sit at
zero-point-four-eight and zero-point-five-zero,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.213
essentially chance.

00:04:31.293 --> 00:04:32.457
In concrete terms,

00:04:32.537 --> 00:04:38.540
RPIembeddor correctly labels nearly three thousand
of the positive interactions and over

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.682
five thousand of the negatives,

00:04:40.762 --> 00:04:44.968
whereas XRPI simply predicts almost everything as
interacting.

00:04:45.048 --> 00:04:50.290
The model clearly learns real signal that
generalizes to unseen RNA families.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.731
To test whether the two embeddings really matter,

00:04:53.811 --> 00:04:58.026
the authors retrain the model after swapping out
the RNA embedding,

00:04:58.106 --> 00:05:02.641
the protein embedding, or both, for random vectors
or one-hot encodings.

00:05:02.721 --> 00:05:06.167
In every one of these variants the model stops working,

00:05:06.247 --> 00:05:08.410
predicting only the negative class,

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:14.885
so its F1 score falls to zero and its accuracy
merely reflects the fraction of negatives in the data.

00:05:14.965 --> 00:05:20.846
Only when both the RNA-FM and ESM-2 embeddings are
present does the model classify correctly.

00:05:20.926 --> 00:05:26.359
This confirms that the foundation-model embeddings
carry the structural and functional

00:05:26.439 --> 00:05:28.410
information the task depends on.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.032
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:31.112 --> 00:05:38.489
RPIembeddor scores a ROC area under the curve of
zero-point-seven-zero on the homology-separated test set,

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:42.710
where the best competitor manages only
zero-point-five-zero.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:48.690
Its F1 of zero-point-five-nine and accuracy of
zero-point-six-seven lead all methods.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.670
The RNAInterAct dataset contributes over one
hundred twenty-two thousand interactions

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.977
spanning nine hundred seventy-six RNA families.

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:04.098
And all of this runs in a compact
one-point-four-million-parameter model powered by the

00:06:04.178 --> 00:06:06.560
RNA-FM and ESM-2 foundation models.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:12.965
The lasting message is that general RNA-protein
interaction prediction from sequence alone

00:06:13.045 --> 00:06:17.509
is achievable when you stand on the shoulders of
foundation models.

00:06:17.589 --> 00:06:24.225
A compact attention network fed with RNA-FM and
ESM-2 embeddings outperforms specialized tools and,

00:06:24.305 --> 00:06:28.227
unlike them, generalizes to RNA families it has
never seen.

00:06:28.307 --> 00:06:32.635
The companion RNAInterAct dataset, split to remove
homology bias,

00:06:32.715 --> 00:06:35.145
gives the community a fair benchmark.

00:06:35.225 --> 00:06:37.180
Both embeddings are essential,

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:42.810
and the authors point toward adding RNA-structure
models and longer sequences next.
